.SH GETTING STARTED
One executable serves three roles: it captures this machine's audio and sends it out (server), it receives audio from another machine and plays it (client), or it just scans the network to see who is already streaming (discover). Without any flag wfas decides on its own: launched from a terminal it prints a short hint, launched by a double click it opens the desktop app.
.SS Entry point
.TP
.B (no flags)
From a terminal, print a short hint. Otherwise open the GUI.
.TP
.B \-\-gui
Open the desktop app.
.RS
.PP
Can be combined with a mode to open the window and start straight away, as in 'wfas \-\-gui \-\-mode server \-\-multicast'.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-cli
Stay in the terminal. Runs an audio server unless another mode is given.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tray
Start the GUI without the Linux tray icon.
.RS
.PP
Use this if the app segfaults inside libgtk\-3 on launch; the tray is the only part that touches GTK. Same as WFAS_NO_TRAY=1, or 'wfas config set ui.linuxTray OFF' to make it permanent.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-tray <auto|on|off>
Override the tray decision for this run.
.br
Default: auto.
.RS
.PP
auto looks for a StatusNotifierItem host on the session bus, which is what KDE Plasma provides on both X11 and Wayland, and falls back to the GTK status icon on X11. Use on to try anyway when the probe cannot reach the bus. If the icon never appears on Wayland the usual cause is a missing AppIndicator library: install libappindicator\-gtk3 on Fedora, libayatana\-appindicator3\-1 on Debian and Ubuntu.
.RE
.SS Modes
.TP
.B \-\-server
Start as audio source. Shorthand for \-\-mode server.
.TP
.B \-\-client
Start as audio receiver. Shorthand for \-\-mode client.
.TP
.B \-\-mode <m>
server | client | discover | rtp | snapcast
.RS
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
server    capture this machine's audio and serve it
client    receive audio from a server and play it
discover  scan the network for active servers, then exit
rtp       listen to an RTP stream, as 'wfas rtp listen' does
snapcast  join a Snapcast server, as 'wfas snapcast listen' does
.fi
.RE
The last two have no counterpart in the window, so \-\-gui does not apply to them. They are described under 'receive'.
.RE
.SS Discovery
.PP
Discovery is passive: servers announce themselves on the multicast group, so nothing is probed or port\-scanned. Each entry reports the transport it was seen on and its address family.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Keep scanning and update the list live instead of exiting after one pass.
.RS
.PP
It applies to discovery and to 'wfas pair invite', the two views that would otherwise print once and exit. Anywhere else there is nothing to keep open, so wfas refuses the flag rather than ignoring it and falling back to starting a server.
.RE
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help server, wfas \-\-help client, wfas \-\-help network.
.SH SERVER OPTIONS
The server captures what this machine is playing and sends it out over the native WFAS protocol. Everything here is about that native stream; the extra protocols that can run alongside it live under 'Streaming protocols'.
.SS Ports and delivery
.TP
.B \-\-port <n>
WFAS streaming port.
.br
Default: 9090.
.TP
.B \-\-mic\-port <n>
Port the microphone return channel comes back on.
.br
Default: 9092.
.TP
.B \-\-multicast
Serve to every listener on the group instead of one unicast peer.
.RS
.PP
Implied by \-\-rtp, \-\-http, \-\-dlna and \-\-snapcast. Note that \-\-auth\-mode applies to unicast only.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-interface <name>
Network interface to bind and announce on.
.br
Default: Auto.
.SS Session lifetime
.TP
.B \-\-persist
Keep the server up when a client disconnects, waiting for the next one.
.br
Default: off: a unicast server exits with its client.
.RS
.PP
A unicast server normally serves one session and exits when that client leaves. With \-\-persist the disconnect ends the session only: the port stays bound, discovery starts announcing again and the next client is served without restarting anything. Made for headless and systemd use, where Restart=always would otherwise bounce the whole process after every normal disconnect. The server then ends only when you end it: q on the terminal, Ctrl\-C, or systemctl stop. Multicast has no per\-client session and already behaves this way, so there the flag changes nothing. To make it the default: 'wfas config set server.persist true', or the switch in Settings > Startup & window. The flag then only matters for a run where the setting is off.
.RE
.SS Capture engine
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mute\-render
Keep playing on this machine's speakers while streaming.
.br
Default: off: local playback is muted for the session.
.RS
.PP
While capturing, the server mutes local playback so the same audio is not heard twice, once from the speakers here and once from the client. What it mutes is the render side, not the capture: the WASAPI render endpoint on Windows, the default PulseAudio/PipeWire sink on Linux, the system volume on macOS. The previous state is restored when the server stops. Pass \-\-no\-mute\-render to leave it alone and hear the audio in both places, e.g. headphones on this machine and earbuds on the phone. Native engine only: with \-\-legacy\-engine capture goes through a virtual cable, which takes the audio off the speakers by itself. To make it the default: 'wfas config set server.muteRender false', or the switch in Settings > Audio Quality. The flag then only matters for a run where the setting is on.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-legacy\-engine
Use the legacy FFmpeg grabber instead of the native C audio engine.
.RS
.PP
The native engine is the default on all platforms:
.RS 2
.nf
  Windows  WASAPI loopback (no virtual driver needed)
  macOS    ScreenCaptureKit
  Linux    PulseAudio/PipeWire via dlopen
.fi
.RE
Use \-\-legacy\-engine on Linux if PulseAudio is unavailable, or for compatibility with older setups. Alias: \-\-no\-native\-engine.
.RE
.SS Session descriptor
.PP
An SDP file describes the stream well enough for VLC, ffplay or any RTP receiver to open it. Only meaningful together with \-\-rtp.
.TP
.B \-\-sdp
Print stream.sdp to stdout when the server starts.
.TP
.B \-\-sdp\-out <path>
Write stream.sdp to a file, for example /tmp/stream.sdp.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help protocols, wfas \-\-help security, wfas \-\-help network.
.SH CLIENT OPTIONS
The client receives a WFAS stream and plays it on a local device. With no \-\-connect it discovers a server by itself and joins the first one it sees.
.SS Connection and output
.TP
.B \-\-connect <ip>
Server address to connect to. Implies client mode.
.br
Default: auto\-discover.
.TP
.B \-\-output <name>
Audio output device name.
.br
Default: system default.
.RS
.PP
Matching is partial and case\-insensitive, so a fragment such as 'Loopback' is enough. Run 'wfas devices' to see the exact names this system reports.
.RE
.SS Playback
.TP
.B \-\-volume <0\-100>
Initial volume percentage.
.br
Default: 100.
.TP
.B \-\-mute
Start muted.
.TP
.B \-\-latency <ms>
Jitter buffer used on the Wi\-Fi link.
.br
Default: 120, range 0\-5000.
.RS
.PP
Larger values survive a noisier network, smaller ones cut the delay. The USB buffer is separate and is set by \-\-usb\-latency. Persists as audio.latencyMs when saved with 'wfas config set'.
.RE
.SS Microphone return channel
.PP
Sends the client's microphone back to the server (talkback), independent of the main server\-to\-client audio stream.
.TP
.B \-\-mic
Enable the microphone return channel.
.TP
.B \-\-mic\-input <name>
Microphone device name. Same partial matching as \-\-output.
.TP
.B \-\-mic\-routing <m>
mix | virtual | off
.br
Default: mix when \-\-mic is given.
.RS
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
mix      blend the mic into the server's captured audio
virtual  expose the mic on the server as a virtual device
off      disable
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SS Audio devices  (wfas devices)
.PP
Lists every audio device Java Sound reports on this system, with the exact name to hand to \-\-output or \-\-mic\-input, what each one can do, and why any of them was skipped. Alias: list\-devices. Add \-\-json for machine\-readable output.
.TP
.B devices
List the audio devices this system reports.
.PP
A device is normally kept only if it answers a format probe. That probe opens the card, so it fails whenever another audio server (PulseAudio, PipeWire, JACK) holds it, which is common on Linux and on Raspberry Pi. When no device passes, wfas keeps the full list instead of leaving you with nothing, and 'devices' says so explicitly.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help receive, wfas \-\-help runtime, wfas \-\-help network, wfas \-\-help config.
.SH STREAMING PROTOCOLS
Besides its own protocol the server can speak four standard ones at the same time, so receivers that have never heard of WFAS can still play the audio. Each of these implies \-\-multicast. None of them is authenticated or encrypted: \-\-auth\-mode, \-\-auth\-key and \-\-encrypt cover the native WFAS stream only.  This is the sending side. wfas also receives two of these protocols \- 'wfas rtp listen' and 'wfas snapcast listen' \- and those live under 'receive'.
.SS RTP
.PP
Plain RTP for VLC, ffplay and hardware receivers. Pair it with \-\-sdp so the receiver knows what it is getting.
.TP
.B \-\-rtp
Enable the RTP protocol. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-port <n>
RTP port.
.br
Default: 9094.
.SS HTTP
.PP
A plain HTTP stream any browser or media player can open by URL.
.TP
.B \-\-http
Enable the HTTP stream. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-http\-port <n>
HTTP port.
.br
Default: 8080.
.TP
.B \-\-http\-safari
Serve Safari\-compatible AAC. Implies \-\-http.
.SS DLNA
.PP
Pushes the audio to the DLNA renderers saved in the settings, so the renderer starts playing without anyone touching it.
.TP
.B \-\-dlna
Push audio to the saved DLNA renderers. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-dlna\-port <n>
DLNA media endpoint port.
.br
Default: 8081.
.TP
.B \-\-dlna\-format <f>
auto | lpcm | wav | mp3 | adts
.br
Default: auto.
.SS Snapcast
.PP
Acts as a Snapcast server for synchronised multiroom audio. Any snapclient on the network (Raspberry Pi, ESP32, Home Assistant, the Snapcast mobile apps) can join and stay in sync.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast
Act as a Snapcast server. Implies \-\-multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-port <n>
Snapcast audio stream port.
.br
Default: 1704.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-control\-port <n>
Snapcast JSON\-RPC control port.
.br
Default: 1705.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-codec <c>
pcm | flac | opus
.br
Default: pcm.
.RS
.PP
flac roughly halves the bandwidth; opus needs 48000:16:2.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-chunk <n>
Chunk size in ms: 10 | 20 | 40 | 60
.br
Default: 20.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-buffer <n>
Client playback buffer in ms.
.br
Default: 1000.
.TP
.B \-\-snapcast\-name <s>
Stream identifier advertised to clients.
.br
Default: default.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help receive, wfas \-\-help server, wfas \-\-help security, wfas \-\-help runtime.
.SH RECEIVING RTP & SNAPCAST
The other direction of 'Streaming protocols': there wfas speaks RTP and Snapcast to send, here it speaks them to receive. 'wfas rtp' plays a stream produced by ffmpeg, VLC, a hardware sender or another wfas server; 'wfas snapcast' joins a Snapcast server as one of its clients, in sync with every other room, and can read and change the whole installation from the control channel. Neither has anything to do with the native WFAS client, which is 'wfas \-\-client'.
.SS RTP  (wfas rtp <command>)
.PP
The source can be given in four ways, and they can be mixed: an .sdp file, the descriptor on standard input, an address, or the name of a source saved earlier. Whatever is given, the individual options below still have the last word, so a saved source can be reused on a different port without editing anything.
.TP
.B rtp listen [source]
Listen to an RTP stream and play it. Aliases: play, receive.
.RS
.PP
source is recognised on its own: a path ending in .sdp or any readable file is an SDP descriptor, \- reads one from standard input, 239.255.0.1:9094 or :9094 is an address, and anything else is looked up among the saved sources by name, by #index or by address. With nothing at all, the single saved source is used.
.RE
.TP
.B rtp inspect <source>
Say what the descriptor means, and what it leaves out. Aliases: check, parse.
.RS
.PP
Reads the SDP without opening a socket, then prints the address, the format, whether the delivery is multicast, and whether the audio can be played natively or has to go through FFmpeg. Anything the descriptor did not say and wfas had to assume is listed as a warning, because those assumptions are exactly what makes a stream come out at the wrong speed or pitch.
.RE
.TP
.B rtp sdp [source]
Print the SDP descriptor of a source, for another player.
.RS
.PP
Writes the original descriptor when the source came from one, and otherwise builds a minimal valid one from the fields. \-\-sdp\-out also saves it to a file.
.RE
.TP
.B rtp sources
List the saved sources with their index. Aliases: list, ls, saved.
.RS
.PP
The index is the #n accepted anywhere a source is named.
.RE
.TP
.B rtp save [name]
Save the source described by the current options.
.RS
.PP
A source with the same address and port replaces the old entry instead of adding a duplicate. Saved sources are shared with the desktop app: they are app.rtpSources in the configuration.
.RE
.TP
.B rtp forget <name|#n|all>
Remove a saved source. Aliases: remove, rm, delete.
.SS RTP options
.TP
.B \-\-sdp\-file <path>
Read the SDP descriptor from a file, or from standard input with \-.
.RS
.PP
Same thing as passing the path as the source; the flag exists so scripts do not have to rely on the guess. Do not confuse it with \-\-sdp and \-\-sdp\-out, which belong to the sending side.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-address <addr>
Multicast group to join, or the local address to listen on.
.br
Default: any interface.
.RS
.PP
An address in 224.0.0.0/4 (or ff00::/8) is a multicast group and is joined on the interface chosen by \-\-interface. Anything else, empty included, means unicast: the port alone decides.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-port <n>
Port to listen on.
.br
Default: 9094, or whatever the SDP says.
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-codec <name>
Payload encoding, as in the a=rtpmap line.
.br
Default: L16.
.RS
.PP
L16 is raw 16\-bit PCM and is played straight through, which is the lowest latency path. Every other encoding is handed to FFmpeg, which decodes it and adds its own buffering.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-rate <hz>
Sample rate of the stream.
.br
Default: 48000, range 8000\-192000.
.RS
.PP
It has to match the sender. A wrong rate does not fail: it plays at the wrong speed and pitch, which is why 'rtp inspect' says out loud when the descriptor did not state one.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-channels <n>
1 or 2.
.br
Default: 2.
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-payload <n>
RTP payload type, 0\-127.
.br
Default: 96.
.RS
.PP
Packets carrying a different payload type are ignored, so this is what keeps two streams sharing one port apart.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-rtp\-name <text>
Label for the source, used when saving and in the listings.
.PP
The output device, the buffer and the initial volume come from the shared options: \-\-output, \-\-latency, \-\-volume and \-\-mute behave exactly as they do for the native client.
.SS Snapcast  (wfas snapcast <command>)
.PP
Snapcast keeps every room on the same sample at the same instant, so this is a full client and not just a player: the control channel shows the groups, the clients and the streams of the whole installation, and lets you change them. Alias: snap. The server can be named as an address, as the name of a saved one, or left out entirely, in which case it is looked for over mDNS and used if it is the only one.
.TP
.B snapcast listen [server]
Join the server and play in sync. Aliases: connect, play, join.
.TP
.B snapcast discover
Find the Snapcast servers announced on this network. Aliases: browse, scan.
.RS
.PP
Add \-\-watch to keep the list updating, \-\-json for one line per server.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast mixer
Join the server and control the whole installation from one screen. Aliases: ui, tui, top.
.RS
.PP
This is 'snapcast listen' with a screen instead of status lines: it plays the audio too, exactly like the desktop application, where listening and controlling are one window.  Arrows or jk move, left and right change the volume by 5, m mutes, g moves the client to another group, s detaches it into one of its own, n renames, y sets the latency, t picks the stream, r refreshes, q leaves. On a group row the volume keys shift every client in it by the same amount, so the balance between rooms is kept.  Everything shown comes from the server, and every key sends a command on the control channel: there is no local state that could drift from what the other apps see. Without an interactive terminal, or with \-\-json, it falls back to the status lines of 'snapcast listen', so a pipe gets output and not a screenful of escape sequences.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast status
Groups, clients and streams of the whole installation.
.RS
.PP
Add \-\-watch to follow it live: the view is redrawn when the server actually reports a change, not on a timer.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast clients | groups | streams
The three views on their own, one row per entry.
.TP
.B snapcast volume <client> <0\-100>
Set a client's volume. Alias: vol.
.RS
.PP
A client is named by its name, by its id, by its address or by the #n of the listing; a fragment of the name is enough as long as it matches only one. The same goes for groups.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast mute | unmute <client>
Mute or unmute one client.
.TP
.B snapcast latency <client> <ms>
Per\-client delay, \-2000 to 2000.
.RS
.PP
Positive values delay that room, to line it up with one that is slower to play.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast rename <client> <name>
Rename a client on the server.
.TP
.B snapcast move <client> <group>
Move a client into an existing group.
.RS
.PP
Snapcast has no move command: the only one available rewrites the whole membership of the destination group, so wfas reads it, adds the client and writes it back. The old group is the server's problem.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast split <client>
Detach a client into a group of its own. Alias: detach.
.RS
.PP
Same mechanism in reverse, and it depends on the server: some do not re\-home a client removed from a group, and leave it invisible while it keeps playing. wfas checks afterwards and puts it back where it was if that happened, rather than leaving you with a room nobody can control.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast group\-mute <group> on|off
Mute or unmute a whole group.
.TP
.B snapcast group\-rename <group> <name>
Rename a group. Alias: group\-name.
.TP
.B snapcast group\-stream <group> <stream>
Point a group at one of the server's streams.
.RS
.PP
\&'wfas snapcast streams' lists the ids that are accepted here.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast servers
List the saved servers with their index. Alias: saved.
.TP
.B snapcast save [name]
Save the server described by the current options.
.RS
.PP
Shared with the desktop app as app.snapcastServers.
.RE
.TP
.B snapcast forget <name|#n|all>
Remove a saved server. Aliases: remove, rm, delete.
.SS Snapcast options
.TP
.B \-\-snap\-host <host>
Address of the Snapcast server to join.
.RS
.PP
Not to be confused with \-\-snapcast, which makes this machine a Snapcast server instead of a client.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-snap\-port <n>
Audio port of the server.
.br
Default: 1704.
.TP
.B \-\-snap\-control\-port <n>
JSON\-RPC control port of the server.
.br
Default: 1705, or the audio port plus one.
.RS
.PP
The control channel is optional: without it the audio still plays, but the volume of this machine cannot be changed from anywhere and the rest of the installation stays invisible.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-snap\-name <text>
Name announced to the server.
.br
Default: this machine's hostname.
.RS
.PP
It is what shows up in the Snapcast apps and in 'wfas snapcast clients'. Unrelated to \-\-snapcast\-name, which names the stream a wfas Snapcast server publishes.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audio
Mixer only: control the system without joining it.
.RS
.PP
Turns 'snapcast mixer' into a remote control. Useful from a laptop that should change the volume of the house without becoming another room, and on a machine with no usable audio output at all.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-snap\-id <id>
Client identifier.
.br
Default: the MAC address of this machine.
.RS
.PP
The server remembers volume, latency and group per identifier, so changing it starts from a clean slate; two clients sharing one identifier fight over the same settings.
.RE
.PP
\-\-volume, \-\-mute and \-\-latency go through the control channel, so the server and every other app see them. Without a control channel \-\-volume and \-\-mute fall back to a local trim, and \-\-latency has nowhere to go.
.SS While listening
.PP
Both sessions take single\-letter commands on standard input, and both answer 'wfas control' from another terminal.
.TP
.B q
Stop and exit. Also quit, stop.
.TP
.B v <0\-100>
Set the volume.
.TP
.B m / u
Mute and unmute.
.TP
.B s
RTP: packets, loss and buffer. Snapcast: codec, buffer and sync error.
.TP
.B g
Snapcast only: the groups and clients of the installation.
.TP
.B l <ms>
Snapcast only: this client's latency.
.PP
\-\-viz draws the spectrum analyser for either one, and 'wfas control status' from another terminal reports what is being received, from where, and how full the buffer is.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help protocols, wfas \-\-help client, wfas \-\-help runtime.
.SH USB & NETWORK
How the audio physically gets across: over Wi\-Fi, over the USB cable, or both, and which address family to use.
.SS USB link
.TP
.B \-\-usb
Stream over the USB cable instead of Wi\-Fi.
.RS
.PP
Enable USB tethering on the Android phone: the phone becomes the gateway and the app finds the link on its own. Lower and far steadier jitter than Wi\-Fi, which is what lets the buffer shrink. Not available on macOS: there is no built\-in RNDIS driver, so Android USB tethering does not come up. Overrides the saved net.usbMode for this run.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-no\-usb
Force the USB link off even if it is enabled in the config.
.TP
.B \-\-usb\-latency <ms>
Jitter buffer used only while the USB link is up. Implies \-\-usb.
.br
Default: 20, range 5\-120.
.RS
.PP
The Wi\-Fi buffer set by \-\-latency is left untouched.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-usb\-iface <name>
Force a specific tethering interface. Implies \-\-usb.
.RS
.PP
Accepts the interface name or its display name as listed by \-\-debug, or 'Auto' to restore automatic detection.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-wfas\-mode <m>
always | not\-on\-usb | off
.br
Default: not\-on\-usb.
.RS
.PP
When this device serves the native WFAS protocol:
.RS 2
.nf
  always      always, over Wi\-Fi and over the cable
  not\-on\-usb  over Wi\-Fi, but suppressed as soon as the USB link
              comes up
  off         never over Wi\-Fi; only the cable and the other
              protocols remain
.fi
.RE
With 'off' and no \-\-rtp/\-\-http and no USB link there is nothing left to serve, and the server refuses to start.
.RE
.SS Interface and address family
.TP
.B \-\-interface <name>
Network interface to bind and announce on.
.br
Default: Auto.
.TP
.B \-\-ip4, \-\-ipv4
IPv4 only: bind 0.0.0.0, announce and listen on 239.255.0.1 alone.
.TP
.B \-\-ip6, \-\-ipv6
IPv6 only: the [ff02::5746] group alone.
.PP
Without either flag the app is dual\-stack: it binds the wildcard, joins both groups, and picks per peer the most usable address, where a routable v4 or v6 beats a link\-local. Force a family only to work around a broken network; on a v6\-only LAN auto already does the right thing.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help server, wfas \-\-help client, wfas \-\-help config.
.SH SECURITY & PAIRING
Everything in this section applies to the native WFAS protocol only. RTP, HTTP, DLNA and Snapcast are standard protocols: their clients are never authenticated and their audio always goes out in the clear, so enabling them alongside \-\-encrypt exposes the very audio you are encrypting.
.SS Authorization and encryption
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-mode <m>
off | ask | key
.br
Default: off, unicast only.
.RS
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
off  accept anyone
ask  prompt on the terminal for each client that connects
key  require the pre\-shared key
.fi
.RE
The security of a command line session comes from the command line, and from nowhere else: a key set in the app protects the app, and 'wfas \-\-server' starts in the clear whatever the app window says. That way one command means one thing on every machine.  The other direction is explicit, so it is allowed: asking for key mode without saying which key looks for one where you may have put it on purpose \- WFAS_AUTH_KEY first, then the system credential store, then this terminal. None found, nothing starts.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-ask\-timeout <sec>
Only with \-\-auth\-mode ask: seconds before an unanswered client is refused.
.br
Default: 60, 0 waits forever.
.RS
.PP
The prompt appears on the terminal, or inside the visualizer panel when \-\-viz owns the screen. No terminal at all, or \-\-json, means nobody can answer: the client is refused and the reason is logged, because a silent yes is not a decision anyone made.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-key <key|\->
Pre\-shared key. Implies \-\-auth\-mode key.
.RS
.PP
The key is never sent on the wire: both sides prove they hold it through a mutual HMAC challenge\-response. A key written inline is readable in the process list for as long as wfas runs, and stays in your shell history: pass \- to read it from standard input instead, or use \-\-auth\-key\-file.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-key\-file <path>
Read the pre\-shared key from a file. Implies \-\-auth\-mode key.
.RS
.PP
The first non\-empty line of the file is the key. Keeps it off the command line and out of your shell history; set the file readable only by you. WFAS_AUTH_KEY does the same through the environment.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt
Encrypt the audio with ChaCha20\-Poly1305.
.RS
.PP
Requires a key, so pair it with \-\-auth\-key, or use \-\-qr to have one generated. See 'wfas \-\-protocol' for the wire details.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-qr
Generate a key, start encrypted, and print the pairing QR.
.RS
.PP
Server mode. Implies \-\-auth\-mode key and \-\-encrypt, so it needs no \-\-auth\-key: the code carries the key it just made. While the server runs, p prints a new invite and r rotates the key.  That key lives in this process and nowhere else: it is never written to the configuration or to the credential store, and it dies with the server. An invite is worth something only while the server that issued it is up, and the next one starts from a key nobody has seen before \- so a device paired once is not paired for good.
.RE
.SS QR pairing  (wfas pair <command>)
.PP
Hands a receiver everything it needs in one shot: address, port and a freshly generated 256\-bit key. The key is never typed and never travels on the wire; the invite carries it and the handshake proves it. Invites last 120 seconds.
.TP
.B pair invite
Generate an invite and draw it as a QR code on the terminal.
.RS
.PP
With a server already running, the invite is generated by that instance so it matches the live session; otherwise it is generated offline and saved to the config, ready for the next 'wfas \-\-server'. Add \-\-watch for a live countdown that renews the code on expiry: press n for a new invite, r for a new key, k to reveal the key, q to quit.
.RE
.TP
.B pair regenerate
Same, but always with a brand new key. Aliases: rekey, new\-key.
.RS
.PP
In multicast this evicts every listener still using the old key, which is exactly what you want after handing the code to the wrong person.
.RE
.TP
.B pair connect <link>
Join using an invite link. Alias: join.
.RS
.PP
Accepts the wifiaudio://pair?... form or the https one. Starts the client with the key already applied.
.RE
.TP
.B pair inspect <link>
Decode a link and show what it contains, without connecting.
.RS
.PP
Exits non\-zero if the invite has expired. Aliases: check, parse. Also available as 'wfas inspect <link>'.
.RE
.TP
.B pair encode <text>
Render any text as a QR code on the terminal. Alias: render.
.RS
.PP
Also available as 'wfas encode <text>'.
.RE
.TP
.B pair status
Show pairing state, key origin and handler registration.
.TP
.B pair off
Turn QR pairing off and restore the manually typed key. Alias: disable.
.TP
.B pair register
Force\-register the wifiaudio:// handler with this OS.
.RS
.PP
Per\-user, no administrator rights. Every run already does this in the background, rewriting the entry when it points at a different executable, so moving or reinstalling the app repairs itself. Use this only to see the repair fail loudly.
.RE
.TP
.B pair unregister
Remove the wifiaudio:// handler.
.SS QR rendering
.TP
.B \-\-no\-qr
Print only the link, no ASCII art.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Two characters per module instead of half\-blocks.
.RS
.PP
Use it if the terminal font has no U+2580/U+2584. Needs twice the width.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-invert
Invert the code for terminals with a light background.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-key
Print the pairing key instead of masking it.
.PP
See also: wfas \-\-help protocols, wfas \-\-help reference.
